<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wars of Alexander the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/336-bce">336</a>–<a href="/years/323-bce">323 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Conquests of Alexander the Great · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> were the campaigns fought by Alexander III of Macedon between 336 and 323 BCE, first to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and then against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Persian Empire</a>. Victories at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus">Granicus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Issus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a> gave him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and he pressed on through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley. His conquests spread Greek rule and culture across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and opened the Hellenistic age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wars-of-alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Wars of Alexander the Great" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_the_Great_mosaic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Alexander became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in 336 BCE, after his father Philip II was assassinated by Pausanias of Orestis, the captain of his bodyguards. He also succeeded Philip as head of the League of Corinth, the confederation of Greek states under Macedonian hegemony that had been formed to oppose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. The news of Philip's death roused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and the Thracian tribes to revolt, and Alexander acted quickly, riding south with 3,000 Macedonian cavalry and going round the Thessalians by crossing Mount Ossa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the spring of 335 BCE Alexander marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and defeated the Triballi and the Illyrian kings Cleitus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glaukias">Glaukias</a>. While he was in the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> rebelled again; the city was razed and its territory divided among the other Boeotian cities, which cowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> into submission. In 334 BCE he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> into Asia. He won at the Granicus River in May 334 BCE, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Issus</a> in November 333 BCE, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> fled the field, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a> in 331 BCE. In 332 BCE he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, the last Persian port to hold out, which held about 40,000 people.</p><p>From 329 BCE he fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and Sogdiana, against Spitamenes and the Sakas, and in 327 BCE his men took the Sogdian Rock by night. There he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a>, whom he later married. In 327 and 326 BCE he fought the hill clans of Gandhara, and Curtius reports that the whole population of Massaga was killed. He defeated the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> at the Hydaspes in 326 BCE, the most costly battle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> fought. At the Hyphasis River his exhausted army refused to go further east, and the river marks the limit of his conquests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Alexander sent part of his army to Carmania under Craterus and a fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nearchus">Nearchus</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and led the rest back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> through the Gedrosian Desert. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, he lost three quarters of his army on the sixty-day march. He had also planned an expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and then a campaign westward against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, but after his death his generals, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a>, abandoned these plans. His empire proved unstable. It broke up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-diadochi">Wars of the Diadochi</a>, which lasted about forty years and belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wars of Alexander the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
